python3-sylkrtc
===============

A client library for SylkServer's WebRTC gateway (the sylkRTC protocol), with
the command line tools built on it. What the gateway gives an account, without a
SIP stack of its own: video rooms (audio, video, chat, cameras), 1 to 1 calls,
and the account's addressbook, the same Sylk Mobile, sylk-desktop and Blink use.

Tools:

  sylk-conference  joins a video room with audio and chat, and publishes local
                   and RTSP (LAN) cameras in it: a headless room client, or a
                   camera bridge
  sylk-session     makes and takes 1 to 1 calls (audio, video, messages, DTMF)
  sylk-addressbook shows and changes the account's addressbook: contacts and
                   groups, cached locally
  sylk-account     shows the account's private storage on SylkServer: the
                   contact avatars and the PGP key escrow
  sylk-journal     the account's message journal on SylkServer: a summary by
                   content type, counted by the server
  sylk-settings    manages their configuration (~/.sylkclient): accounts,
                   general settings, sessions, RTSP and local cameras

Modules (package sylkrtc):

  discovery   SylkServer discovery: the _sylkserver.<domain> TXT record, the
              domain's configuration document (wsServer, iceServers, conference
              and guest domains), cached per domain
  rooms       room addresses: links, room@domain, room names; RoomTarget
  connection  the sylkRTC-2 WebSocket to a webrtcgateway (twisted + autobahn)
  videoroom   joining a video room: publisher, feeds, participants, moderation,
              chat, extra videos; independent of the media
  call        the account at the gateway: registration, 1 to 1 calls (its
              session API: placing, taking, answering, DTMF, messages), and
              the addressbook requests
  addressbook the addressbook (contacts, groups, policies) and its local cache
  gst         the media with GStreamer webrtcbin (audio publisher, feeds' audio
              mixed to the speakers, extra videos from RTSP, V4L2 or a test source;
              CallMedia: a call's audio and video both ways)
  device      who the tools are to SylkServer: one device for all of them
              (User-Agent, device id and name)
  serverapi   the account's HTTPS API (account storage, message journal), with
              the API token
  cli         what the command line clients share
  sipconfig   finding the account to use in ~/.sylkclient, ~/.sipclient and ~/.blink
              (the same SIP SIMPLE configuration format), read only
  sdp         SDP helpers
  settings    the sylk clients' settings in the SDK's configuration (sylk-settings)
  onvif       finding cameras on the LAN (WS-Discovery) and their RTSP address (ONVIF)
  rtsp        checking that an RTSP camera answers (DESCRIBE, Basic or Digest auth)
  v4l2        this machine's video capture devices (sysfs and VIDIOC_QUERYCAP)

The sylkRTC protocol has implementations in JavaScript for the other Sylk
clients; this package is the Python one:

  sylkrtc.js               https://github.com/AGProjects/sylkrtc.js
                           desktop and web: sylk-desktop, https://github.com/AGProjects/sylk-webrtc
  react-native-sylkrtc.js  https://github.com/AGProjects/react-native-sylkrtc.js
                           mobile: Sylk Mobile, https://github.com/AGProjects/sylk-mobile
  SylkServer (the gateway) https://github.com/AGProjects/sylkserver

Everything runs in the twisted reactor thread and reports with the
NotificationCenter of python3-application; logging goes to the 'sylkrtc' logger.
A client with another media stack (libwebrtc on macOS) gives VideoroomSession its
own media factory (see sylkrtc/videoroom.py for what it is told and tells back).


Using the library in a client
-----------------------------

The server keeps the list of the devices that use an account (sylk-account
devices, the Devices tab of Blink's Data manager). Every account-add says which
device sends it, so a client names itself:

    session = VideoroomSession(identity, room, ws_server, media_factory,
                               user_agent='Blink 6.2.0 (Linux)',
                               device_id=instance_id, device_name='Blink Qt on ag-xps (Linux)')
    account = Account(identity, ws_server, user_agent=..., device_id=..., device_name=...)
    connection = Connection(url, user_agent=..., verify_tls=..., device_id=..., device_name=...)
    api = ServerAPI(program, account_info, token, verify_tls, user_agent=..., device_id=..., device_name=...)

device_id is the client's own stable id (Blink: its SIP instance id), device_name
is for humans, without a version (the version belongs in user_agent). Without
them the tools' identity is used (below), and its id is read from, or made in,
~/.sylkclient/config. One connection is kept per wsServer URL (Connections): the
first session or account that opens it gives its arguments, so a process is
expected to be one client.

Nothing is registered at the gateway unless Account.register() is called: a
client that registers over SIP itself (Blink) only adds the account, so the
gateway does not fork its calls and messages. The library runs in the twisted
reactor thread and posts its notifications there; logging goes to the 'sylkrtc'
logger, for the client to forward to its own log.


The tools as a device
---------------------

All the tools are one device to the server: python-sylkrtc on <host>, with the
User-Agent python-sylkrtc <version>, the same over HTTPS (X-Sylk-Device-Id,
X-Sylk-Device-Name) and over the WebSocket (account-add deviceId, deviceName).
The id is the general setting device_id of ~/.sylkclient/config, made at the
first use of any tool (sylk-settings --general show lists it). The file
~/.sylkclient/device_id that sylk-account kept before is taken over once, so its
entry on the server carries on. Changing device_id makes the tools a new device.


sylk-conference
---------------

Joins a room with audio and chat over WebRTC, without SIP, and publishes up to
four cameras in it as videos of the same participant (the gateway feature
multiple-video-feeds).

Its own folder is ~/.sylkclient/ (created when missing). Its config file is in
the SIP SIMPLE configuration format, where indentation matters: manage it with
sylk-settings, as sip-settings3 does for ~/.sipclient. It holds:

    SylkSettings:                       general
        audio_input = none              ; auto, none or a GStreamer source description
        audio_output = none             ; auto, none or a GStreamer sink description
        muted = false
        tls_verify_server = false
        default_session = myroom

    Sessions:                           what a room is joined with, by room: one session per room
        myroom:                         a room name, room@domain or a room link
            name = "My home conference"
            account = alice@example.com ; else the default account
            cameras = Garden, Desk      ; by name or id
            muted = true                ; overrides the general setting (audio_input, audio_output too)

    RTSPCameras:                        network cameras, usable by any session
        default:                        credentials of the RTSP cameras without their own
            user = admin
            password = secret
        a4-14-37-0b-cc-12:              its MAC address when known (cameraN otherwise)
            name = Garden
            model = "HIKVISION DS-2CD3345-I"   ; what the camera calls itself, set by discover
            url = rtsp://192.168.1.20/stream1
            transcode = auto            ; auto (relay H264 when the room is H264), yes, no
            resolution = 640x360        ; when encoded here
            fps = 10

    LocalCameras:                       video devices of this machine, usable by any session
        local1:
            name = Desk
            device = /dev/video0
            enabled = false
        local2:
            name = Test
            pattern = ball              ; a GStreamer test pattern instead of a device

    Accounts:                           accounts of its own (the SDK's)

Accounts are searched in ~/.sylkclient, then in the SIP SIMPLE clients'
~/.sipclient/config and Blink's ~/.blink/config (read only, for credentials): the
one named with -a or by the session, else the default account of the first that
has one. sylk-settings --account import copies credentials from them, --rtsp-camera
discover adds the ONVIF cameras that answer on the LAN (their RTSP address asked
over ONVIF with the credentials given, or those of the cameras already
configured; for Hikvision, Dahua, Reolink and Axis the usual address when ONVIF
refuses), --rtsp-camera check tells whether each camera answers.

A camera's id is its MAC address when it can be learned (the neighbour table, or
ONVIF): discover then recognises a camera whose IP address changed and updates its
address, check says where its MAC address answers now, and sylk-conference retries a
failed camera at that address. --rtsp-camera identify gives existing cameras their MAC
address as id. RTSPCameras: default holds the credentials of the cameras that have
none of their own (in their settings or their address); discover tries them first
and does not copy them into the cameras it adds (--rtsp-camera defaults user= password=).

Options: sylk-settings -g general, -s sessions, -r RTSP cameras, -c local cameras,
-a accounts, -d another configuration folder. sylk-conference -r and -c publish RTSP
and local cameras besides the session's: without a value all the enabled ones of
that kind (sylk-conference -r myroom works too), or one configured by
name or id, or LABEL=SOURCE; -d another folder for the account, -v debug, -t
sylkRTC messages.

Our own video is a local camera: by default the one attached, an external (USB)
camera when one is plugged in (its USB port is removable), else the built-in one;
without any, we publish audio only. -c alone (or -c all) uses every camera
attached, -c NAME a configured one, -c none (false, 0) none. The first local camera
is our video; the RTSP cameras (-r) and any other local one are published under
it, so the others see one participant with its cameras grouped.

What is joined: the session given on the command line by its room or its name,
else the default session; a room without a session is joined with the general
settings and no cameras. Command line options override the session, which
overrides the general settings; -r and -c add cameras.

Examples:

    sylk-settings --account import alice@example.com
    sylk-settings --general set audio_input=none audio_output=none
    sylk-settings --rtsp-camera add Garden rtsp://192.168.1.20/stream1 user=admin password=secret
    sylk-settings --local-camera add Desk /dev/video0
    sylk-settings --local-camera discover           (this machine's video capture devices, by their stable /dev/v4l/by-id path)
    sylk-settings --rtsp-camera discover                 (ONVIF cameras on the LAN, added with their RTSP address)
    sylk-settings --rtsp-camera discover user=admin password=secret add=no
    sylk-settings --rtsp-camera check [name]             (does each camera answer, with its credentials)
    sylk-settings --rtsp-camera defaults user=admin password=secret
    sylk-settings --rtsp-camera identify                 (MAC addresses as ids)
    sylk-settings --session add myroom name="My home conference" cameras=Garden,UTE
    sylk-settings --session set myroom room=otherroom      (moves the session to another room)
    sylk-settings --session default myroom
    sylk-conference --server-info
    sylk-conference                        (the default session)
    sylk-conference myroom                 (or sylk-conference "My home conference")
    sylk-conference myroom                         (audio and the attached camera: external, else built-in)
    sylk-conference -r -c myroom                   (every enabled RTSP camera and every camera attached)
    sylk-conference myroom -c none                 (audio only)
    sylk-conference myroom -i bob -i alice@example.org,carol   (invites them once in the room; bob means bob@<the account's domain>)
    sylk-conference -r myroom --auto-disconnect 0     (stays: by default it leaves and exits after 10 minutes in the room)
    sylk-conference https://example.com/conference/other -c "Test=test"

Every minute (--stats-interval, 0 for never) it logs each stream's bitrate,
bytes, round trip time and loss (from the RTCP reports), and the totals; leaving,
a summary of what the whole run sent and received.

What it shows on the console is also written, with a timestamp on each line, to
~/.sylkclient/logs/sylk-conference-YYYY-MM-DD.log, with what is typed (chat and
commands) as "> text".

It leaves the room and exits 10 minutes after getting in (--auto-disconnect
SECONDS, 0 to stay, as a camera bridge run by systemd would).

Typed lines go to the room's chat; /mute, /unmute, /hand, /who, /invite user[@domain] ..., /quit.
Without a terminal (--no-input, or run by systemd) it only joins, publishes and
logs. A room that ends or a connection that drops is joined again; a camera that
fails is started again, both with a growing delay up to a minute.

RTSP cameras: an H264 camera is relayed as it is when the room's video codec is
H264 (nothing decoded, fit for a Raspberry Pi), otherwise it is decoded and
encoded to the room's codec at the given resolution. Relayed video keeps the
camera's own key frame interval, so set it short (1-2 s) in the camera, or
others wait that long to see it; set the camera's bitrate below the room's limit
too, as relayed video does not adapt to the network. transcode = yes always
encodes, transcode = no only relays.


sylk-session
------------

Calls someone, or takes calls, over WebRTC: the gateway places the SIP call for
the account (session-create; account-register and incoming-session for incoming
calls). It uses the account, general settings and local cameras of
sylk-conference.

    sylk-session alice                  (calls alice at the account's domain)
    sylk-session alice@example.org
    sylk-session alice -c none          (no video of ours; theirs is still shown)
    sylk-session                        (registers and waits for calls, asking y/n for each)
    sylk-session --auto-answer 2        (answers by itself after 2 s)

Audio both ways; our video is the camera attached (an external one when plugged
in, else the built-in one), -c NAME a configured local camera, -c none none.
Their video goes to the SIP SIMPLE clients' video window (sipclient.video of
sipclients3) when it is installed, else to a GStreamer window when there is a
display (--video-output window, auto, none or a GStreamer sink description),
scaled to 1280x720 with its aspect kept (--video-size WxH, or native).

The terminal takes single keys, no Enter; h or ? lists the ones that apply. Waiting:
c call someone (then type user or user@domain), / type a command, q quit. A call
ringing in: y accept, n reject; one ringing in during a call asks the same, y
hanging up the current call first. Calling: x hang up. In a call: m mute/unmute,
s RTP statistics (per stream rate, bytes, round trip time, loss, then the
totals), i call info, x hang up, 0-9 * # DTMF (SIP INFO), Enter to type a message
to the far end. The commands typed after /: /answer, /reject, /hangup,
/call user[@domain], /mute, /unmute, /dtmf DIGITS, /stats, /quit. A call
placed from the command line exits when it ends; waiting for calls goes on after
each one. Ctrl-C hangs up the call (rejects one ringing in); Ctrl-C again, or out
of a call, quits. --auto-disconnect SECONDS hangs up that long after the call is
established (default: no limit). Bandwidth, round trip time and loss are logged
every 10 s (--stats-interval) and on /stats, and shown over the video window;
the console log is in ~/.sylkclient/logs/sylk-session-YYYY-MM-DD.log.


sylk-account
------------

Shows the account's private storage on SylkServer
(/webrtcgateway/storage/<account>): the per-account object store where the
clients keep the contact avatars (namespace avatars) and the PGP key escrow
(keys/pgp-private).

    sylk-account                               the namespaces, usage and limits
    sylk-account list NAMESPACE                its objects: size, type, modified, who wrote it, etag
    sylk-account NAMESPACE                     the same (sylk-account keys)
    sylk-account devices                       the devices that used the account: first and
                                               last use with IP (forget one: delete devices ID)
    sylk-account get NAMESPACE ID [-o FILE]    one object (text and JSON printed)
    sylk-account delete NAMESPACE ID [-y]      remove one object (asks first; only the version shown)

It talks HTTPS with the account's API token. The gateway does not hand that
token to a WebSocket client, so it is taken from a client configuration that
keeps it (Blink: ~/.blink/config), or given with --token. The server is the
one the domain's SylkServer discovery names. -j prints the server's JSON.


sylk-journal
------------

The account's message journal on SylkServer, the history the clients sync
from.

    sylk-journal                               how many entries, of which content
                                               types, in and out, oldest and newest
    sylk-journal contacts [-n N]               per contact: entries in and out
    sylk-journal type TYPE [-n N]              one content type per contact (TYPE or part
                                               of it: location, imdn, file-transfer)
    sylk-journal files                         the files the server keeps: sent and
                                               received, by type, size, age, largest
                                               conversations, and when they expire

The server counts (GET /webrtcgateway/messages/summary/<account> and
/transfers/summary/<account>): nothing is downloaded. The token, the server and the device headers are those of
sylk-account (sylkrtc.serverapi). -j prints the server's JSON.


sylk-addressbook
----------------

Shows and changes the account's addressbook: the one SylkServer keeps (XCAP) and
Sylk Mobile, sylk-desktop and Blink share. It is fetched through the gateway and
cached in ~/.sylkclient/addressbook/<account>.json; showing it reads the cache
(fetched when there is none, or with -f). A change fetches it anew, sends the
change and updates the cache with what the server stored. The gateway gives the
addressbook to a registered account only, so the account is registered while
connected (a few seconds).

    sylk-addressbook                           the contacts, then the groups
    sylk-addressbook -f                        fetched anew
    sylk-addressbook contacts [TEXT]           the contacts (whose name or address has TEXT)
    sylk-addressbook groups | policies
    sylk-addressbook show CONTACT|GROUP
    sylk-addressbook show -c [CONTACT]         the contacts, or one
    sylk-addressbook show -g [GROUP]           the groups, or one with its contacts
    sylk-addressbook --check [-f]              what Blink and Sylk Mobile would repair
    sylk-addressbook contact add NAME URI [URI...]
    sylk-addressbook contact rename CONTACT NAME
    sylk-addressbook contact add-uri CONTACT URI | remove-uri CONTACT URI
    sylk-addressbook contact delete CONTACT
    sylk-addressbook group add NAME | rename GROUP NAME | delete GROUP
    sylk-addressbook group add-contact GROUP CONTACT... | remove-contact GROUP CONTACT...

A URI is user (at the account's domain), user@domain, a number, or TYPE=URI
(mobile=+31612345678); addresses and types are stored lowercase, a type is
sip or tel when none is given. CONTACT and GROUP are an id, a name, a unique part of a
name (not for delete), or for a contact one of its addresses; show lists all
the matches of a name that is not unique. The lists show
the ids and are sorted by id (-s name, or -s uri for contacts). The server
stamps each change with who made it (modified_by, modified_agent:
sylk-addressbook).

--check changes nothing. It lists its rules, numbered R1, R2 ... in a fixed
order, with their result (pass, or the number of findings), then the findings. It applies the repairs Blink for macOS (Cocoa), Blink
Qt and Sylk Mobile (with blink-addressbook.js, which sylk-desktop uses) make
when they start to the cached addressbook in memory, and shows, for every
contact or group concerned, what each of them would write: group kinds, duplicate
groups and contacts, groups not on their reserved id, the Calls group,
videoconference. addresses, phone numbers, broken addresses (Phone=..., tel=...), names that only echo an address,
duplicate addresses, Tel and Conference, Blocked and the presence policies,
Deleted, presence allowed without a subscription, rooms in Messages, defaults
and members that are not contacts. A finding marked ! is one where the clients
would write different things. Some rules are decided rather than taken from a
client, and their findings show the Wanted result above what the clients do: a
conference contact is never in Messages, Tel and Conference hold only phone
numbers and rooms, SIP addresses and address types are lowercase, no address is Bonjour's or on
a LAN host (an instance id, a private, loopback or link-local IP, .local: XCAP
is shared, Bonjour has none and such an address works on one LAN only; it is
removed, with its contact when it has no other), and contacts sharing an address are one
contact (the lowest id, with all the addresses and the groups of all; not
Deleted unless all of them were, not Messages for a room, Blocked only if you
say so). The report ends with a chapter for each client:
for each rule they disagree on, what it does now and what would have to change
in it for the three to agree. The rules depend on the conference server (--conference-domain,
default conference.sip2sip.info) and the dial plan (--replace-leading-zero,
--idd-prefix); what depends on a device's history or address book is left out.
